From: Shirogane Tsumugi <artoriapendragon123@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/sun4i: backend: add dedicated suniv F1C100s quirks

The suniv DT node lists "allwinner,suniv-f1c100s-display-backend"
ahead of the "allwinner,sun4i-a10-display-backend" fallback, but
sun4i_backend has no entry for it, so the F1C100s/F1C200s DEBE runs
with the A10 quirks and inherits supports_lowest_plane_alpha = false.

That flag guards an erratum of the early SoCs where the lowest plane
of pipe 0, if it carries any alpha, discards its pixel data and shows
the background color instead. With it unset, atomic_check rejects any
configuration whose lowest enabled plane has a non-opaque global
alpha, and allows only one alpha-carrying plane in total.

The epass layer layout is:

  layer 0 (zpos 0): video, tiled NV12 through the DEFE frontend
  layer 1 (zpos 1): overlay, ARGB8888, faded via the plane "alpha"
                    property (parked at 0 = fully transparent)
  layer 2 (zpos 2): UI, RGB565

While the video layer is enabled every commit passes: the lowest
plane is opaque NV12. But any commit that disables layer 0
renormalizes zpos and the ARGB overlay with alpha < 0xFFFF becomes
the lowest plane -> -EINVAL. The kernel generates exactly such a
commit by itself in atomic_remove_fb(), when userspace removes a
framebuffer still bound to the video plane: the plane-only disable
is rejected by the quirk check, and the disable-the-CRTC retry is
rejected too because the internal state has allow_modeset = false
(drm_atomic_check_only() refuses the implied modeset). Observed on
F1C200s as:

  WARNING: CPU: 0 PID: 9 at drivers/gpu/drm/drm_framebuffer.c:991
  atomic remove_fb failed with -22
    drm_mode_rmfb_work_fn -> drm_framebuffer_remove -> atomic_remove_fb

with the plane left scanning out a framebuffer that no longer has an
ID. The same check would also reject legitimate future configs
(e.g. overlay fade-in while the video layer is off).

The lowest-plane-alpha erratum is only documented for the first
DEBE generation (A10/A13/A20 quirks all leave the flag unset); the
suniv blocks are later cuts of the IP (its VE is the A31-generation
core, see the cedrus suniv patch). Assume the DEBE matches the
A23/A33 behaviour and give suniv its own quirks with
supports_lowest_plane_alpha = true, which also lifts the alpha-plane
budget from 1 to 2.

That assumption is the one thing to verify on silicon: disable the
video plane, give the ARGB overlay a global alpha below 0xFFFF, and
check that its pixels still blend instead of collapsing to the
background color, e.g.

  modetest -M sun4i-drm -s <conn>@<crtc>:<mode> \
    -P <overlay_plane>@<crtc>:<w>x<h>@AR24 -w <overlay_plane>:alpha:32768

If the erratum does reproduce, flip supports_lowest_plane_alpha back
to false and keep only the dedicated match entry.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun4i/sun4i_backend.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun4i/sun4i_backend.c
@@ -1000,6 +1000,10 @@
 static const struct sun4i_backend_quirks sun9i_backend_quirks = {
 };
 
+static const struct sun4i_backend_quirks suniv_backend_quirks = {
+	.supports_lowest_plane_alpha = true,
+};
+
 static const struct of_device_id sun4i_backend_of_table[] = {
 	{
 		.compatible = "allwinner,sun4i-a10-display-backend",
@@ -1029,6 +1033,10 @@
 		.compatible = "allwinner,sun9i-a80-display-backend",
 		.data = &sun9i_backend_quirks,
 	},
+	{
+		.compatible = "allwinner,suniv-f1c100s-display-backend",
+		.data = &suniv_backend_quirks,
+	},
 	{ }
 };
 MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(of, sun4i_backend_of_table);
